@Bestmotion2389 You don't need to edit the story-boards, though...? They're... storyboards... for *concept* work. Not first draft, *concept.* You brainstorm, you throw spaghetti at the wall (the AI), and you see what sticks, then you take the scenes or designs or environments or animations that seem like they have something going for them, and you manually first-draft them with human artists (can even trace if you do genuinely want it to be part of the drafting pipeline, then you just trace layer-by-layer over the generated image, giving it the underlying layer and mask structure you want). But again, not using it as part of the drafting process, and instead just concept and brainstorming, instead of having to *manually draft every concept piece and story-board piece,* you can generate the concepts and story-boards in *minutes or less.* You can do the same thing with keyframe brainstorming. You wanna try some different poses or expressions or angles or perspectives or fields of view or whatever for your keyframe, but you don't want to manually re-pose and squeeze and stretch your character 18 times going through every project layer? *GET THE AI TO DRAFT A FLAT MOCKUP TO SEE IF YOU LIKE THE POSES FIRST, THEN MANUALLY RE-POSE THE ACTUAL PROJECT FILE.* The issue isn't that you're using "the wrong AI", it's that "you're using the AI wrong." You're trying to frame it as *the one tool to the rule them all* instead of using it *as one of the tools in your toolbox that can support your creative process and save you oodles of time.*

And yeah, it doesn't understand movement. *Yet.* It's AI, not just a basic I/O machine. The more we use the tools, the more feedback we give it, the more mistakes we tell it were made, the better its fidelity gets. Check image/animation generation from 4 years ago against the animations in this video. You're really going to sit here and tell me that there hasn't been *magnitudes* of improvement? AI three years ago *couldn't figure out hands.* Now it can generate fairly convincing hyper-realistic art and make animations that--while kinda shoddy and not particularly engaging--are *functional, repeatable, and recognizable examples* of what the prompts asked for. I'm not saying this is great work by human animator standards, but I am saying it's phenomenal progress for a machine that couldn't remember how many fingers were supposed to be on each hand less than 5 years ago. And relating back to my penultimate point here; therefore, I believe that setting up a video that goes out of its way to downplay the capabilities of AI is absurd.

DALL-E's first iteration came out in *2021* and we have since taught these electric rocks to make an old man dance as a cartoon *IN LESS THAN 5 YEARS OF ACTIVE AI DEVELOPMENT, THROUGH A GLOBAL HEALTH CRISIS.* You know any 4-5 year old humans busting out animations like this? If you do, they're considered *prodigies of their craft.* The AI *isn't even 5 years old* and you're shitting on it for not understanding the underlying physics of movement yet. Really, truly, genuinely, think about that for more than thirty seconds before you type your next response ♥

